TypeScript Interface Patterns (Internal Wiki): In TypeScript, interfaces define the shape of objects without runtime overhead. For optional properties with defaults, use the Partial<T> utility type or provide defaults in the constructor. Example:

interface UserConfig {
  name: string;
  roles: string[];
  settings: Record<string, unknown>;
}

function createUser(config: Partial<UserConfig> = {}): UserConfig {
  return {
    name: config.name ?? 'anonymous',
    roles: config.roles ?? ['viewer'],
    settings: config.settings ?? {},
  };
}

This avoids mutable default issues since each call creates a new object literal. TypeScript's structural typing means these interfaces can be extended via intersection types without inheritance.

JavaScript Object Patterns (Team Style Guide, v3.1): When creating objects with default values in JavaScript, avoid sharing mutable references. The spread operator provides shallow copies:

const DEFAULT_OPTIONS = Object.freeze({
  retries: 3,
  timeout: 5000,
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
});

function createClient(overrides = {}) {
  return { ...DEFAULT_OPTIONS, ...overrides };
}

For nested objects, use structuredClone() (available in Node 17+) or a deep-clone utility. Note: Object.freeze is shallow; nested objects remain mutable unless individually frozen.

Angular HTTP Client Guide (v17 Documentation): Angular provides the HttpClient service for making HTTP requests. Inject it via the constructor and use it in lifecycle hooks:

@Component({ selector: 'app-users', template: '<ul><li *ngFor="let u of users">{{u.name}}</li></ul>' })
export class UsersComponent implements OnInit {
  users: User[] = [];
  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}
  ngOnInit(): void {
    this.http.get<User[]>('/api/users').subscribe({
      next: (data) => this.users = data,
      error: (err) => console.error('Failed to load users', err),
    });
  }
}

The OnInit lifecycle hook fires after the component's inputs are initialized. For cleanup, implement OnDestroy and unsubscribe from observables to prevent memory leaks.

Angular Signals and RxJS (v17 Migration Notes): Angular 17 introduces signals as a reactive primitive. While RxJS Observables remain supported, signals offer simpler reactivity for many use cases:

export class DashboardComponent {
  private dataService = inject(DataService);
  metrics = signal<Metric[]>([]);
  loading = signal(true);

  constructor() {
    effect(() => {
      this.dataService.getMetrics().subscribe(m => {
        this.metrics.set(m);
        this.loading.set(false);
      });
    });
  }
}

Signals provide fine-grained change detection without Zone.js, improving performance in large applications.

Payment Gateway Integration (API Reference v2.4): The payment processing module handles transaction routing and settlement. Key endpoints:

POST /api/v2/payments/charge
Body: { amount: number, currency: string, payment_method_id: string, idempotency_key: string }
Response: { transaction_id: string, status: 'pending'|'completed'|'failed', created_at: string }

POST /api/v2/payments/refund
Body: { transaction_id: string, amount?: number, reason: string }
Response: { refund_id: string, status: 'processing'|'completed', estimated_completion: string }

Webhook events are sent for status transitions. Configure webhook URLs in the dashboard under Settings > Integrations. All amounts are in the smallest currency unit (cents for USD).

Inventory Management Module (inventory/stock.py): The stock management system tracks warehouse quantities and triggers reorder alerts.

def check_stock_level(product_id: str, warehouse_id: str) -> StockStatus:
    """Check current stock for a product in a specific warehouse."""
    current = db.query(Stock).filter_by(
        product_id=product_id, warehouse_id=warehouse_id
    ).first()
    if not current:
        return StockStatus(available=0, reserved=0, reorder_needed=True)
    reorder_point = current.reorder_threshold or 10
    return StockStatus(
        available=current.quantity - current.reserved,
        reserved=current.reserved,
        reorder_needed=current.quantity <= reorder_point,
    )

The module integrates with the procurement service via an async event bus. When stock drops below the reorder threshold, a RestockRequested event is published.

Express.js Authentication Middleware (Node.js Tutorial): Express middleware functions have access to the request, response, and next objects. JWT authentication middleware:

const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function authenticateToken(req, res, next) {
  const authHeader = req.headers['authorization'];
  const token = authHeader && authHeader.split(' ')[1];
  if (!token) return res.sendStatus(401);
  jwt.verify(token, process.env.ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET, (err, user) => {
    if (err) return res.sendStatus(403);
    req.user = user;
    next();
  });
}

Apply globally via app.use(authenticateToken) or per-route. Passport.js provides strategy-based authentication (local, OAuth, JWT) as an alternative.

Spring Security Configuration (Java/Spring Boot): Spring Security uses filter chains for authentication. JWT configuration in Spring Boot 3:

@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {
  @Bean
  public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    return http
      .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
      .sessionManagement(sm -> sm.sessionCreationPolicy(STATELESS))
      .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
        .requestMatchers("/api/auth/**").permitAll()
        .anyRequest().authenticated())
      .addFilterBefore(jwtAuthFilter(), UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)
      .build();
  }
}

The JwtAuthFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter and validates tokens on each request.
